# ADR-015: Adopt typed WorkflowState dataclass

## Status

Accepted

## Context

The Coordinator passed runtime state between its lifecycle methods as
a dict[str, Any]. Every method that read or wrote state needed to know
the exact key strings ("invoice_amount_eur", "po_responsible_person",
"consultations_used", and so on). The contract was implicit. Key typos
were silent failures. mypy could not help.

## Decision

Replace the dict with a dedicated dataclass, WorkflowState, in
core/workflow_state.py. Every field is documented as a property of
the dataclass. Default values handle the "not yet populated" case
(Optional[X] = None for fetched data; 0 for counters; empty list for
the approval recipient list; False for the booked flag).

## Consequences

Positive: mypy --strict can verify state access. IDE autocomplete
guides correct field use. The state contract is documented in one
file. Migrations of the Coordinator and follow-up refactors (the
tool lifecycle move) became materially safer.

Negative: state access changes from dict subscript to attribute
access. A one-time migration touched ~30 lines in pipeline.py. No
other module needed changes because state lives entirely inside
Coordinator.run() and its lifecycle methods.

## Alternatives considered

TypedDict was considered. Rejected because it does not support
default values for non-Required keys cleanly, and because the
team's mental model of state is closer to "a record with fields"
than "a dict whose keys happen to be known".